1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,478
Rabbits breed. Foxes eat rabbits. When rabbits are plentiful, foxes thrive.

2
00:00:05,478 --> 00:00:10,298
Then rabbits get scarce, foxes go hungry, and the rabbits recover.

3
00:00:10,298 --> 00:00:13,512
The two populations rise and fall in cycles.

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:19,640
Now imagine many species, their interactions organised by a tree-shaped network.

5
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:23,228
Here are two for four species: a chain and a star.

6
00:00:23,228 --> 00:00:26,672
Could they secretly behave exactly the same way?

7
00:00:26,672 --> 00:00:27,820
Pause and guess.

8
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,319
The answer is no, apart from rare special cases.

9
00:00:31,319 --> 00:00:35,477
And one thing first: someone else announced this result earlier.

10
00:00:35,477 --> 00:00:39,960
What you'll see is an independent check of it, not a first discovery.

11
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:45,055
Start with rabbits alone, growing by about ten percent a month.

12
00:00:45,055 --> 00:00:50,033
A hundred become about a hundred and ten, then about a hundred and twenty-two.

13
00:00:50,433 --> 00:00:55,608
The rate of change is the gradient of this graph, written d r by d t.

14
00:00:55,608 --> 00:01:01,233
Here it is nought point one times r: growth proportional to the population.

15
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:07,774
Now add foxes. The more foxes there are, the slower the rabbits grow.

16
00:01:07,774 --> 00:01:11,900
The rabbits' rate of change is r, times two minus f.

17
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:18,060
And foxes need food. Their rate of change is f, times r minus three.

18
00:01:18,433 --> 00:01:23,249
To see the whole story at once, use a plane: rabbits across, foxes up.

19
00:01:23,249 --> 00:01:28,753
Each point is a possible state, and its arrow shows where that state is heading.

20
00:01:29,133 --> 00:01:32,353
Follow the arrows and you go round a loop: the cycle.

21
00:01:32,353 --> 00:01:35,997
These are the Lotka-Volterra equations, about a century old.

22
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:42,433
With more species, each follows the same kind of rule: its population, times a weighted

23
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:47,765
sum of all the populations. There are no separate birth or death numbers, so every term

24
00:01:47,765 --> 00:01:49,604
is two populations multiplied.

25
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,507
The weights fit in a table. Row one says how species one responds to each species.

26
00:01:55,507 --> 00:01:58,664
This four-species table is our running example.

27
00:01:59,033 --> 00:02:04,241
The big negative numbers on the diagonal mean that each species crowds itself out.

28
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:10,218
Now the network. Draw each species as a dot and join some pairs with lines.

29
00:02:10,218 --> 00:02:14,711
With no loops, and every dot reachable from every other, it's a tree.

30
00:02:14,711 --> 00:02:18,293
Four dots allow just two trees: the chain and the star.

31
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:24,622
Careful: the tree is not a food web. In these systems every species can affect every

32
00:02:24,622 --> 00:02:29,515
other. Instead, the tree is a rule about which effects must be equal.

33
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:35,450
Here's the rule. Remove species two, and the chain falls into branches: one on one side,

34
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:40,748
three and four on the other. Species two must affect everything in a branch equally.

35
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:46,588
In the table, that's column two: a one for species one, and a two for both three and

36
00:02:46,588 --> 00:02:51,914
four. Every column follows this rule, leaving ten free numbers instead of sixteen.

37
00:02:51,914 --> 00:02:54,381
All such tables form the chain family.

38
00:02:54,767 --> 00:02:59,946
The star gives its own pattern. Removing the centre leaves three branches, so column

39
00:02:59,946 --> 00:03:01,919
one has three different numbers.

40
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,225
To picture this, suppose species three and four are absent.

41
00:03:07,225 --> 00:03:12,167
They stay absent, since their rate of change is zero times something.

42
00:03:12,167 --> 00:03:16,464
So we can watch species one and two on a plane, with arrows.

43
00:03:16,833 --> 00:03:21,876
Now describe the same motion in new variables: mixtures of the old ones.

44
00:03:21,876 --> 00:03:27,129
Keep x one, and replace x two by twenty-seven x two minus twenty-six x one.

45
00:03:27,129 --> 00:03:32,241
The grid shears, the arrows go with it, but only the description changed.

46
00:03:32,633 --> 00:03:36,692
Usually, the new rules look nothing like our equations.

47
00:03:36,692 --> 00:03:42,744
But this time they have the same shape again: each variable, times a weighted sum.

48
00:03:42,744 --> 00:03:44,441
Only the table changed.

49
00:03:44,833 --> 00:03:50,663
When mixing turns one system exactly into another, the two are called equivalent: the

50
00:03:50,663 --> 00:03:56,425
same system in disguise. So the real question is: can a chain system be disguised as

51
00:03:56,425 --> 00:03:57,385
a star system?

52
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:03,703
In twenty twenty-five, Peter van der Kamp conjectured no, for any two different trees,

53
00:04:03,703 --> 00:04:06,119
and checked it up to eight species.

54
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:12,360
Why did that particular mixture work? Pause and think about it.

55
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:19,393
Start simple. An absent species stays absent, because its rate of change is itself times

56
00:04:19,393 --> 00:04:24,085
something. Anything with that property, once zero, stays zero.

57
00:04:24,467 --> 00:04:29,948
Our mixture, twenty-seven x two minus twenty-six x one, is one of these.

58
00:04:29,948 --> 00:04:36,875
Work out its rate of change, and it factorises: the mixture itself, times minus twenty-five

59
00:04:36,875 --> 00:04:39,083
x one minus twenty-six x two.

60
00:04:39,467 --> 00:04:43,741
So on the line where the mixture is zero, the motion stays on that line.

61
00:04:43,741 --> 00:04:48,788
Start species one and two in the ratio twenty-seven to twenty-six, and they keep that

62
00:04:48,788 --> 00:04:49,619
ratio forever.

63
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,927
Quantities like this are called Darboux polynomials: hidden invariants.

64
00:04:54,927 --> 00:04:58,674
They aren't conserved themselves, but their zeros are.

65
00:04:58,674 --> 00:05:02,768
And our mixing simply turned this hidden line into an axis.

66
00:05:03,167 --> 00:05:09,618
A tree system has one for each species, and one for each tree line, mixing just its two

67
00:05:09,618 --> 00:05:15,106
ends; the tree rule makes that work. The chain has four plus three: seven.

68
00:05:15,106 --> 00:05:16,367
So does the star.

69
00:05:17,467 --> 00:05:23,526
Here's why they matter. Mixing can't create or destroy a hidden invariant; it just rewrites

70
00:05:23,526 --> 00:05:26,923
each one. So equivalent systems have matching sets.

71
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:33,433
But mixing scrambles the formulas. After this mixing, x three minus x four has become

72
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:38,700
plain y one. You can no longer tell which invariants were single species.

73
00:05:39,067 --> 00:05:45,252
So what survives? Take x one, x two, and twenty-seven x two minus twenty-six x one.

74
00:05:45,252 --> 00:05:50,394
The third is built from the other two, and stays so after any mixing.

75
00:05:50,394 --> 00:05:53,971
Call three invariants like this a linked triple.

76
00:05:54,367 --> 00:05:59,227
Each tree line gives one linked triple: its two ends and their mixture.

77
00:05:59,227 --> 00:06:05,114
Draw each as a triangle. Mixing can move everything around, but it never changes which

78
00:06:05,114 --> 00:06:06,415
triples are linked.

79
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:11,561
Now count. In the chain, two invariants sit in two triangles each.

80
00:06:11,561 --> 00:06:17,692
In the star, one sits in three. Mixing can't change that, so a chain system can never

81
00:06:17,692 --> 00:06:19,856
be disguised as a star system.

82
00:06:20,933 --> 00:06:25,427
But there's a hole. What if there are hidden invariants we haven't found?

83
00:06:25,427 --> 00:06:28,997
Extra ones could form extra triangles and spoil the count.

84
00:06:29,367 --> 00:06:31,708
First, a mixture can't skip a species.

85
00:06:31,708 --> 00:06:36,945
Mix species one and three but not two: species two affects one with strength one, but

86
00:06:36,945 --> 00:06:40,704
three with strength two, since they're in different branches.

87
00:06:40,704 --> 00:06:42,183
That mismatch breaks it.

88
00:06:42,567 --> 00:06:48,577
Next, try three in a row: amounts p one, p two and p three of species one, two and three.

89
00:06:48,577 --> 00:06:54,183
Writing out the condition gives one equation per pair, with numbers from the table.

90
00:06:54,567 --> 00:07:00,533
Take the first equation, subtract twenty-six times the second, and add the third.

91
00:07:00,533 --> 00:07:04,142
The p two terms cancel. The p three terms cancel.

92
00:07:04,142 --> 00:07:07,383
What's left is fifty-five p one equals zero.

93
00:07:07,767 --> 00:07:11,583
But p one isn't zero: species one is in the mixture.

94
00:07:11,583 --> 00:07:16,866
Contradiction. Bigger mixtures contain three in a row, so they fail too.

95
00:07:16,866 --> 00:07:21,783
The only hidden invariants are single species and the tree's pairs.

96
00:07:22,167 --> 00:07:28,868
That fifty-five comes from column two: one plus twenty-six, plus two plus twenty-six.

97
00:07:28,868 --> 00:07:32,494
The proof needs sums like this to be non-zero.

98
00:07:32,494 --> 00:07:36,279
Generic means ruling out such rare coincidences.

99
00:07:36,667 --> 00:07:41,994
This cancellation, with letters in place of numbers, was checked line by line in Lean,

100
00:07:41,994 --> 00:07:43,915
a program that verifies proofs.

101
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,458
An invariant in two or more triangles is a species with several neighbours.

102
00:07:49,458 --> 00:07:54,629
A triangle joining two of those is a line between them; a triangle with one adds an end

103
00:07:54,629 --> 00:07:55,104
species.

104
00:07:55,500 --> 00:08:00,378
Here it is on a seven-species tree, starting from triangles alone.

105
00:08:00,378 --> 00:08:04,000
The rule rebuilds the tree exactly, for any size.

106
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,508
So generic systems from different trees are never equivalent.

107
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:14,864
Now the history. These tree families were introduced in twenty twenty-three by Quispel,

108
00:08:14,864 --> 00:08:19,704
Tapley, McLaren and van der Kamp. The conjecture followed in twenty twenty-five.

109
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:24,739
In September twenty twenty-six, a preprint by Zijian Zeng announced this result.

110
00:08:24,739 --> 00:08:30,073
It has since been withdrawn and its text is unavailable, so the arguments can't be compared.

111
00:08:30,073 --> 00:08:32,508
Withdrawal doesn't erase the announcement.

112
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:38,337
This work gives an independent, self-contained proof of a closely matching theorem.

113
00:08:38,337 --> 00:08:43,316
It is a verification note, not a first discovery, and it claims no priority.

114
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:49,488
The written proof covers every size. Exact computer checks passed for all nine hundred

115
00:08:49,488 --> 00:08:55,141
and eighty-six trees up to twelve species and, at chosen numbers, the full invariant

116
00:08:55,141 --> 00:08:57,698
lists for two hundred trees up to ten.

117
00:08:57,698 --> 00:08:59,852
Consistency checks, not a proof.

118
00:09:00,233 --> 00:09:02,833
Lean checks only the three-in-a-row step.

119
00:09:02,833 --> 00:09:08,033
The rest is a written proof, reviewed internally, but not yet by outside referees.

120
00:09:08,433 --> 00:09:13,829
There are limits. Special tables are excluded: if every row is the same, every mixture

121
00:09:13,829 --> 00:09:19,162
is an invariant and the tree is lost. And the tree is a rule about equal effects, not

122
00:09:19,162 --> 00:09:20,793
a food web read from data.

123
00:09:21,867 --> 00:09:25,967
To recap. One: each tree defines a family of population systems.

124
00:09:25,967 --> 00:09:31,412
Two: their hidden invariants form linked triples, one per tree line, and mixing can't

125
00:09:31,412 --> 00:09:36,921
change how they overlap. Three: the overlaps rebuild the tree, so different trees give

126
00:09:36,921 --> 00:09:38,715
genuinely different systems.

127
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:44,475
Still open: what exactly happens at the special tables, where extra invariants appear?

128
00:09:44,475 --> 00:09:48,100
And which networks beyond trees can be recovered this way?
